INT. FAMILY MINIVAN - PARKED OUTSIDE AN AMUSEMENT PARK - DAY

Hot sun on the windshield. A cooler on the back seat. A crumpled map with a coffee ring. The air conditioner wheezes.

LORENZO CASTELLANOS, 40s, in the driver's seat, grips the wheel and stares ahead. His wife, ABIGAIL, 40s, sits beside him with a crossword she is not doing. In the back, their daughter, PEPPER, 11, kicks the seat in front of her in steady, bored rhythm.

                    PEPPER
Are we going in?

                    LORENZO
In a minute.

                    PEPPER
You said that ten minutes ago.

                    LORENZO
And I meant it ten minutes ago.

Abigail glances at him. Says nothing. She taps her pencil on the page.

                    PEPPER
Mom, are we going in?

                    ABIGAIL
Your father is thinking.

                    PEPPER
About what?

                    ABIGAIL
About whether we can afford it.

                    LORENZO
That's not what I said.

                    ABIGAIL
It's what you meant.

                    LORENZO
I meant I'd like a minute.

                    ABIGAIL
You've had eleven.

He releases the wheel. She sets down the crossword. Pepper stops kicking.

                    PEPPER
Is something wrong?

                    LORENZO
No.

                    ABIGAIL
Yes.

Lorenzo shoots her a look. She holds it.

                    ABIGAIL (CONT'D)
She's old enough.

                    LORENZO
She's eleven.

                    ABIGAIL
She's eleven and she has eyes.

                    PEPPER
What's going on?

                    LORENZO
Nothing's going on. Daddy just lost his job on Tuesday.

Silence. The air conditioner wheezes. Pepper's face goes very still.

                    PEPPER
Oh.

                    LORENZO
It's going to be fine.

                    PEPPER
Did you do something?

                    LORENZO
No. They closed the whole plant.

                    PEPPER
Everyone?

                    LORENZO
Everyone.

                    PEPPER
So it wasn't you.

                    LORENZO
It wasn't me.

Pepper nods. Studies her hands. After a beat she unbuckles, climbs between the front seats, and wedges herself into the gap, pressing her head against his shoulder.

                    PEPPER
Can we still go in?

                    LORENZO
We have the tickets.

                    ABIGAIL
We paid for them in March.

                    PEPPER
Then it's free.

                    LORENZO
That's not how that works.

                    PEPPER
It's how it works for me.

He starts to laugh. It's cracked, half a sob, and Abigail puts a hand on his arm. Pepper looks between them.

                    PEPPER (CONT'D)
Dad, are you crying?

                    LORENZO
No. I'm sweating. It's hot.

                    PEPPER
You're crying.

                    LORENZO
A little.

She wipes his cheek with her sleeve. He lets her. Abigail drops her head back against the seat and laughs, wet and helpless.

                    ABIGAIL
All right. Everyone out.

                    LORENZO
What about the cooler?

                    ABIGAIL
Leave it.

                    PEPPER
Can I ride the big one?

                    LORENZO
The big one?

                    PEPPER
The one with the loop.

                    LORENZO
You're not tall enough.

                    PEPPER
I'll stand on my toes.

                    LORENZO
They measure at the ankle.

                    PEPPER
(grinning)
Then I'll cheat.

The three of them clamber out. The doors slam. Pepper takes off across the lot at a dead run. Lorenzo and Abigail follow, side by side, his hand reaching for hers.

Halfway there, Abigail stops dead. Turns.

                    ABIGAIL
Did you lock the van?

Lorenzo looks at the keys. At the van. Back at her.

                    LORENZO
Does it matter?

She thinks about it. Starts to laugh again, and they run after their daughter, leaving the minivan wide open under the sun.
